---
/**
 * CandidacyLedgerBlock — the `candidacy_ledger` layout: the "am I a candidate?"
 * band. Two prose columns on the left, a numbered ledger card on the right.
 *
 * EXTRACTED, NOT DESIGNED. Two live bands, and they are the same band:
 *
 *   src/pages/implant-dentistry/index.astro:475-522   #candidacy
 *   src/pages/cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:510-565  #candidacy
 *
 * Element for element the two are identical — `.section.dark` > `.wrap` >
 * `.section-head`, then `.cand-grid` holding `.cand-copy` (h3 / p / h3 / p /
 * `.cand-actions`) beside `<aside class="ledger">` (a `.ledger-head` and a run
 * of `.ledger-row`s, each a `.ledger-num`, an unclassed copy cell and a
 * `.ledger-tag`). The class names are the pages' own on purpose, for the reason
 * CardGridBlock.astro:6-12 records: the page sheets still style them until the
 * stylesheet pass removes their copies.
 *
 * ── THE NUMERALS ARE NOT A FIELD ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * `.ledger-num` is the row's POSITION, computed here. A number an editor can
 * type is a number that goes out of sequence the first time a row is dragged,
 * and this band's whole claim is that the list is a list.
 *
 * They are NOT zero-padded. The eight live rows spell them "1".."4"
 * (implant-dentistry/index.astro:195-198, cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:132-160)
 * — unlike the process/tech grids, which spell theirs "01".."06" and which
 * ProcessStepsBlock and TechGridBlock therefore `padStart`. Checked in the
 * templates rather than assumed from the sibling blocks, because the two
 * conventions live four files apart and read identically in a summary.
 *
 * ── THE TWO SHEETS DISAGREE ABOUT THE LEDGER'S SKIN ──────────────────────
 * Unlike TechGridBlock's two cards — where the skins differ because the GROUNDS
 * differ, charcoal vs sage-pale, and the difference can ride the band value —
 * both candidacy bands sit on `--vs-sage-pale` and still ship different chrome:
 *
 *   cosmetic-dentistry.css:734-786  translucent white card (50% over the pale
 *                                   green), 70%-white border, a full
 *                                   `--vs-sage` numeral, a sage-outlined tag.
 *   implant-dentistry.css:1072-1151 the same geometry in neutral black-alpha:
 *                                   4%-black card, 12%-black border, a
 *                                   10%-black numeral with ink text, a
 *                                   grey-on-grey tag.
 *
 * Same ground, same markup, two skins: that is drift, not a design, and one
 * component cannot carry both — there is no field, and inventing a "skin"
 * select would be a second control for a decision the band already makes.
 * THIS BLOCK SHIPS THE COSMETIC SKIN, because the white-glass-on-sage-pale
 * treatment is the family's answer for this ground everywhere else it appears:
 * TechGridBlock.astro:318-321 documents the same 50%-white/70%-border card as
 * what the sage-pale band takes, and general-dentistry.css:856-858 says its
 * `.pay-card` borrowed "glass-on-sage card chrome ... from candidacy .ledger
 * style". implant-dentistry.css's black-alpha copy is the outlier.
 *
 * NAMED CONSEQUENCE, so nobody discovers it in a screenshot: when the implant
 * hub is wired to this block, its ledger card changes colour — white glass and
 * sage numerals instead of grey. No words move; the word-level diff that grades
 * this phase stays empty. If the practice wants the grey back, the fix is a
 * skin field on the layout, not a second copy of this file and not a page-sheet
 * rule (a page sheet cannot reach anything this block draws — see
 * docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md and the `.section`-qualified selectors below).
 *
 * The breakpoint at which a row stacks is the same disagreement in miniature:
 * cosmetic-dentistry.css:940 collapses `.ledger-row` to two columns at 991px,
 * implant-dentistry.css:1435 waits until 780px. 991px is taken, because
 * cosmetic's tags are the long ones ("Whitening · Veneers") and the earlier
 * collapse is the value that cannot overflow on either page's content.
 *
 * ── `aria-label` ON THE ASIDE ────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Both templates name the ledger for screen readers, and neither label is the
 * visible eyebrow: "Signs you're a candidate for dental implants" against an
 * eyebrow of "Signs you're a candidate", "Common signs cosmetic dentistry is
 * for you" against "Signs this is for you". So it is not derivable, and it is
 * accepted as `ledgerLabel` — a prop the PHP does not have yet. Blank, it falls
 * back to the visible eyebrow, which names the region correctly if less fully;
 * blank as well and the attribute is omitted rather than emitted empty, because
 * `aria-label=""` is worse than no label at all. THE WIRING BATCH SHOULD ADD A
 * `ledger_label` FIELD — this is the one string on the band an editor cannot
 * otherwise reach, and the two live values are recorded above.
 *
 * ── NO `.vs-link` RULE LIVES HERE ────────────────────────────────────────
 * Both copy bodies carry inline `<a class="vs-link">` (the implant band links
 * bone grafting and sinus lift mid-sentence; the cosmetic band links the
 * membership plan), which is why they render `set:html`. The link's colour is
 * left to global.css:39-51 and to each hub sheet's own sage-pale tint
 * (cosmetic-dentistry.css:227-237, implant-dentistry.css:356-366), for the
 * reason ComparisonCardsBlock.astro:922 and TechGridBlock.astro:34-41 record: a
 * rule authored here would be a compound that outranks both and would take the
 * site's own link treatment away from a band that already had it right. The
 * default `.vs-link` — ink with a sage underline — is legible on sage-pale, so
 * unlike MediaSplitBlock's charcoal case there is nothing to rescue.
 *
 * ── OPTIONAL EVERYTHING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every field is optional and a blank one draws nothing, never an empty
 * wrapper: no head without head copy, no `.cand-copy` without copy, no
 * `.ledger` without ledger content, no `.cand-grid` when neither column has
 * anything, and no band chrome at all when the whole row is blank — a
 * never-opened row renders a bare `<section>`. The one wrapper that is NOT
 * conditional is the unclassed `<div>` inside a `.ledger-row`; it is column two
 * of `auto 1fr auto`, so dropping it would move the tag into the copy's cell.
 * That is structure, not a wrapper around a field.
 *
 * HREF POLICY: `ctaHref`/`ctaHref2` store an anchor (#consult), a site path
 * (/dental-membership-plan/), or one of the tokens "book" | "phone" | "map"
 * resolved from src/data/contact.ts through ./cta.ts. Never a pasted
 * booking/tel/maps URL — those live in one place in the site precisely so they
 * cannot go stale in a CMS field. This layout has no `cta_note`: neither
 * template draws a line under the buttons, and a field with no markup behind it
 * reads as supported.
 *
 * Spec: docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md Phase 4, Wave A.
 */
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import { resolveCta } from "./cta";

/**
 * One row of the layout's `ledger` repeater. Every sub-field is optional at the
 * schema level because ACF returns null for anything an editor never filled in;
 * typing them as plain strings would lie to the filter below.
 */
export interface CandidacyLedgerRow {
  /** The `<h5>` — "Healthy adults of any age". */
  title?: string | null;
  /** The `<p>` under it. Plain prose on all eight live rows. */
  body?: string | null;
  /** The pill at the row's end — "CBCT", "Aligners", "Systemic OK". */
  tag?: string | null;
}

interface Props {
  /** DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. Absent when blank. */
  id?: string;
  /** The same value as `id`. Accepted so the spread in PageBlocks is lossless. */
  anchor?: string | null;
  /** Rail label. Read by the page that builds the rail, not by this band. */
  navLabel?: string | null;
  band?: string | null;
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>`, and nothing else (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md, Fact 3). */
  heading?: string | null;
  body?: string | null;

  /** Left column, first pair. The body may carry `<a class="vs-link">`. */
  copyHeading?: string | null;
  copyBody?: string | null;
  /** Left column, second pair. Both live bands fill it; both may still be blank. */
  copyHeading2?: string | null;
  copyBody2?: string | null;

  /** The ledger card's own small label — not the section head's. */
  ledgerEyebrow?: string | null;
  /** The `<h4>` under it. Plain text on both live bands. */
  ledgerHeading?: string | null;
  /**
   * The screen-reader name for the ledger region. See the header: no PHP field
   * supplies it yet, and it falls back to `ledgerEyebrow`.
   */
  ledgerLabel?: string | null;
  ledger?: (CandidacyLedgerRow | null)[] | null;
  /**
   * Which ledger skin this band wears: "sage" (the default) or "ink".
   *
   * A FIELD BECAUSE NOTHING DERIVABLE SEPARATES THE TWO LIVE BANDS. Both
   * /cosmetic-dentistry/ #candidacy and /implant-dentistry/ #candidacy are
   * `section.section.dark`, so the band value cannot tell them apart, yet the
   * sheets skin them differently: cosmetic-dentistry.css:734-740/762-770/780-786
   * is a translucent-white card with a sage numeral bubble, while
   * implant-dentistry.css:1072-1078/1114-1122 is a black-tinted card with an
   * ink numeral. Shipping one skin for both would silently repaint a live band,
   * and a word-level diff — the test this migration is graded on — would not
   * catch it. Blank = "sage".
   */
  ledgerSkin?: string | null;

  /** The six CTA fields. This layout has no note — see the header. */
  ctaLabel?: string | null;
  ctaHref?: string | null;
  /** Hover override. Blank falls back to the destination table in ./cta.ts. */
  ctaHover?: string | null;
  ctaLabel2?: string | null;
  ctaHref2?: string | null;
  ctaHover2?: string | null;
}

/**
 * band value -> the modifier class the page's markup already uses.
 *
 * Read this map, not an ancestor class. `.section.alt` is charcoal green on
 * both hub sheets but cream on teeth-whitening.css:237 (Fact 4), so a block
 * that inherited its band would be wrong on some page the moment a section is
 * dragged. `paper` maps to nothing because a paper band is a bare
 * `class="section"` in every template.
 */
const BAND_CLASS: Record<string, string | undefined> = {
  paper: undefined,
  cream: "cream",
  "sage-pale": "dark",
  sage: "sage",
  charcoal: "alt",
};

const {
  id,
  /* `paper` and not `sage-pale`: it is what the PHP field stores as its own
     default_value (vs-content-model.php, block_preamble()), so a row an editor
     has never opened renders the band wp-admin is showing them. Unreachable for
     any saved row — ACF returns the stored choice — which is exactly why it
     must not invent a different one. */
  band = "paper",
  eyebrow,
  heading,
  body,
  copyHeading,
  copyBody,
  copyHeading2,
  copyBody2,
  ledgerEyebrow,
  ledgerHeading,
  ledgerLabel,
  ledger,
  ledgerSkin,
  ctaLabel,
  ctaHref,
  ctaHover,
  ctaLabel2,
  ctaHref2,
  ctaHover2,
} = Astro.props;

const bandKey = typeof band === "string" && band !== "" ? band : "paper";
const bandClass = BAND_CLASS[bandKey];

const text = (input: unknown): string =>
  typeof input === "string" ? input.trim() : "";

/**
 * The eyebrow carries `light` on the two bands the templates spell
 * `.section.dark` / `.section.sage` — which is where both live candidacy bands
 * sit, so the class is in their HTML and has to be in ours. It is INERT under
 * the current cascade (`.section.dark .eyebrow` at (0,3,0) already beats
 * `.eyebrow.light` at (0,2,0)); it is reproduced because this phase is verified
 * by an HTML diff, not by a rendering. Same rule and wording as
 * ProcessStepsBlock.astro:143-152 and TechGridBlock.astro:146-154.
 */
const eyebrowLight = bandKey === "sage-pale" || bandKey === "sage";

const eyebrowText = text(eyebrow);
const headingHtml = text(heading);
const bodyText = text(body);
const hasHead = eyebrowText !== "" || headingHtml !== "" || bodyText !== "";

const copyHeadingText = text(copyHeading);
const copyBodyHtml = text(copyBody);
const copyHeading2Text = text(copyHeading2);
const copyBody2Html = text(copyBody2);

/* The token map, hover table and resolvers live in ./cta.ts — ONE copy for
   every block that draws a CTA row. The tables only work because the blocks
   agree about what "book" resolves to and what hovering it promises. */
const cta1 = resolveCta(ctaHref, ctaHover);
const cta2 = resolveCta(ctaHref2, ctaHover2);

/**
 * The row draws for EITHER button, not just the first — a band that fills only
 * the second pair must not silently lose it. The LABEL is the switch, matching
 * every sibling block and the PHP field's own promise that leaving both labels
 * blank draws no button row at all.
 */
const ctaLabelText = text(ctaLabel);
const ctaLabel2Text = text(ctaLabel2);
const hasCtaRow = ctaLabelText !== "" || ctaLabel2Text !== "";

/**
 * Button variants, and the one place this block departs from the family map.
 *
 * Every sibling writes `isDarkBand = charcoal || sage` and pairs sage/ghost
 * elsewhere — which treats sage-pale as a light ground. Both live candidacy
 * bands ARE sage-pale and both ship `light` + `outline-light`
 * (implant-dentistry/index.astro:497-500, cosmetic-dentistry/index.astro:535-543),
 * so the family map would change the live HTML on the two pages this block
 * exists to draw. The pairing is therefore keyed off the two grounds where a
 * white pill would disappear — paper and cream — and everything else takes the
 * light pair, which is what both templates ship.
 *
 * `outline-light` and not the family's `ghost-light` for the same reason: it is
 * the variant in the live markup, and the two render differently.
 */
const lightGround = bandKey === "paper" || bandKey === "cream";
const primaryVariant = lightGround ? "sage" : "light";
const secondaryVariant = lightGround ? "ghost" : "outline-light";

const hasCopy =
  copyHeadingText !== "" ||
  copyBodyHtml !== "" ||
  copyHeading2Text !== "" ||
  copyBody2Html !== "" ||
  hasCtaRow;

const ledgerEyebrowText = text(ledgerEyebrow);
const ledgerHeadingText = text(ledgerHeading);
const hasLedgerHead = ledgerEyebrowText !== "" || ledgerHeadingText !== "";

/**
 * Rows with nothing in them are dropped rather than drawn.
 *
 * ACF returns a repeater row an editor opened and abandoned as a row of empty
 * strings, and an empty `.ledger-row` is a visible 44px band of padding between
 * two hairlines. Filtering here is also what keeps the numerals honest: the
 * position below is the position in THIS list, so a blank row between 2 and 3
 * does not print 1, 2, 4.
 *
 * A repeater of nothing but blank rows leaves the `.ledger` unemitted unless it
 * has head copy of its own, and an empty repeater draws no `.cand-grid` column
 * at all — never an empty card.
 */
const rows = (Array.isArray(ledger) ? ledger : []).filter(
  (row) =>
    text(row?.title) !== "" || text(row?.body) !== "" || text(row?.tag) !== "",
);

const hasLedger = hasLedgerHead || rows.length > 0;

/** Blank rather than empty: `aria-label=""` names the region nothing. */
const ledgerLabelText = text(ledgerLabel) || ledgerEyebrowText;

/* Blank = the sage skin, which is what cosmetic-dentistry ships and what four
   of the five other blocks' translucent-card chrome matches. See the prop. */
const skinClass = String(ledgerSkin ?? "").trim() === "ink" ? "skin-ink" : "skin-sage";

const hasGrid = hasCopy || hasLedger;

/**
 * A blank anchor is NO attribute, not an empty one. PageBlocks.astro always
 * hands over a deduplicated id or nothing, so this is unreachable through the
 * CMS — but `id=""` is what Astro emits for an empty string, and a bare `id`
 * in the markup is a difference an HTML diff would report and an editor could
 * never explain.
 */
const sectionId = text(id) || undefined;
---

{/* `data-vs-reveal-batch` on the section, exactly as both templates carry it.
    animations.js animates the section's DIRECT children, which is the one
    `.wrap` — a single fade of the whole band, not a per-row stagger. Moving it
    to `.cand-grid` or the `.ledger` would be a different animation. */}
<section class:list={["section", bandClass]} id={sectionId} data-vs-reveal-batch>
  <div class="wrap">
    {
      hasHead && (
        <div class="section-head">
          {eyebrowText && (
            <span class:list={["eyebrow", eyebrowLight && "light"]}>{eyebrowText}</span>
          )}
          {headingHtml && (
            <h2>
              <Fragment set:html={headingHtml} />
            </h2>
          )}
          {bodyText && <p>{bodyText}</p>}
        </div>
      )
    }

    {
      hasGrid && (
        <div class="cand-grid">
          {hasCopy && (
            <div class="cand-copy">
              {copyHeadingText && <h3>{copyHeadingText}</h3>}
              {copyBodyHtml && <p set:html={copyBodyHtml} />}{
                /* Flush against the previous conditional, no newline between
                   them. Astro preserves whitespace between expressions, so a
                   conditional on its own line adds a permanent whitespace-only
                   text node for every band that fills only the first pair.
                   Same idiom as CardGridBlock's `body2`. */
                copyHeading2Text && <h3>{copyHeading2Text}</h3>
              }{
                copyBody2Html && <p set:html={copyBody2Html} />
              }{
                /* Same flush, same reason: a copy column with no buttons must
                   not leave a text node where the actions row would be. */
                hasCtaRow && (
                  <div class="cand-actions">
                    {ctaLabelText && (
                      <Button
                        variant={primaryVariant}
                        href={cta1.href}
                        hoverLabel={cta1.hover}
                      >
                        {ctaLabelText}
                      </Button>
                    )}{
                      /* Flushed so a band with only the first button adds no
                         whitespace node inside the inline-flex row. */
                      ctaLabel2Text && (
                        <Button
                          variant={secondaryVariant}
                          href={cta2.href}
                          hoverLabel={cta2.hover}
                        >
                          {ctaLabel2Text}
                        </Button>
                      )
                    }
                  </div>
                )
              }
            </div>
          )}{
            /* Flushed against the copy column: a ledger-only band must not open
               with a whitespace text node in the two-column grid. */
            hasLedger && (
              <aside class:list={["ledger", skinClass]} aria-label={ledgerLabelText || undefined}>
                {
                  /* The label and the `<h4>` stay on ONE line each and the
                     second is flushed against the first: broken across lines,
                     the eyebrow's own text would pick up the indentation, and
                     this eyebrow is an `inline-block` — unlike the section
                     head's inline-flex one — whose leading space is not
                     reliably collapsed away. The comment is OUTSIDE the
                     `<header>` for the same reason. */
                  hasLedgerHead && (
                    <header class="ledger-head">
                      {ledgerEyebrowText && <span class:list={["eyebrow", eyebrowLight && "light"]}>{ledgerEyebrowText}</span>}{
                        ledgerHeadingText && <h4>{ledgerHeadingText}</h4>
                      }
                    </header>
                  )
                }{
                  /* Flushed against the head: a ledger with rows and no head
                     copy starts flush at its first row, as an unheaded card
                     would be drawn by hand. */
                  /* The numeral is the row's POSITION and is NOT padded — the
                     eight live rows spell it "1".."4". This comment sits
                     OUTSIDE the row for the reason TechGridBlock records:
                     inside it, Astro emits the surrounding newlines as a
                     whitespace text node the two templates do not have. */
                  rows.map((row, i) => (
                    <div class="ledger-row">
                      <span class="ledger-num">{String(i + 1)}</span>
                      <div>
                        {text(row?.title) && <h5>{row?.title}</h5>}{
                          text(row?.body) && <p>{row?.body}</p>
                        }
                      </div>{
                        text(row?.tag) && <span class="ledger-tag">{row?.tag}</span>
                      }
                    </div>
                  ))
                }
              </aside>
            )
          }
        </div>
      )
    }
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ══ WHERE THESE RULES COME FROM ═══════════════════════════════════════════
     Two source sheets, because this block draws two live bands — and both of
     them are the SAGE-PALE band, which is why nothing below rides the band
     value the way TechGridBlock's card skin does:

       src/styles/pages/implant-dentistry.css   `.impd`
       src/styles/pages/cosmetic-dentistry.css  `.cdent`

     Each page's local `--sage` / `--ink` / `--line` / `--r-xl` / `--f-display`
     aliases are resolved back to the `--vs-*` tokens they alias one-for-one
     (implant-dentistry.css:24-45, cosmetic-dentistry.css:34-55). Rendered
     values are unchanged.

     Where the two sheets disagree — the ledger's skin, and the breakpoint at
     which a row stacks — the cosmetic sheet is the source and the header of
     this file says why, at length. Every such rule is marked `(cosmetic only)`
     with the implant lines it replaces, so the choice is auditable from here.

     ── WHY EVERY BAND RULE IS QUALIFIED WITH `.section` ────────────────────
     Astro's attribute scoping compiles `.ledger` to `.ledger[cid]` —
     specificity (0,2,0), which TIES with the page sheets' own `.impd .ledger`
     and `.cdent .ledger`, and a tie is decided by bundle order. The house rule
     is never to build on that tie, so every rule below is written
     `.section .ledger` → `.section[cid] .ledger[cid]` at (0,4,0), which clears
     both page rules outright. Nothing here depends on which stylesheet a
     bundler happens to emit first.

     The shell primitives (`.wrap`, `.eyebrow`, `h2`, `p`, `.section-head`) are
     left unqualified, as in every sibling block: those DO tie with the page
     sheets, and it is harmless because both sides declare the same values —
     `.impd .wrap` and `.cdent .wrap` are 1240px/40px and so is this. A rule
     whose two candidates differ is never left on a tie. */

  /* ---------- band shell ----------
     `.section` owns its padding and has no margin at all, so this band cannot
     collapse into whichever band an editor drags above or below it. Same rule
     as FinalBand.astro and every sibling block.
     padding: implant-dentistry.css:307-309 = cosmetic-dentistry.css:199.
     `cream` and `sage` are in neither hub sheet — neither page places those
     bands — so their values come from tokens.css via the block family's shell
     (FaqBlock.astro), which is the contract the band select promises. */
  .section {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 110px 0;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
  }
  .section.cream {
    background: var(--vs-cream);
  }
  /* cosmetic-dentistry.css:216 / implant-dentistry.css:333-335 */
  .section.dark {
    background: var(--vs-sage-pale);
  }
  .section.sage {
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  /* cosmetic-dentistry.css:200 / implant-dentistry.css:310-312 */
  .section.alt {
    background: var(--vs-charcoal-green);
  }

  /* Keep the heading clear of the stacked global Nav + sticky TOC when a rail
     chip is clicked. implant-dentistry.css:300-303 (written there as
     `section[id]`); cosmetic-dentistry.css:195. Both custom properties are set
     on the page wrapper and inherit down; the fallbacks are the desktop
     measurements those sheets declare (implant-dentistry.css:25-26). */
  .section[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h, 77px) + var(--toc-h, 68px) + 12px);
  }

  /* implant-dentistry.css:48-52 = cosmetic-dentistry.css:58 */
  .wrap {
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  /* implant-dentistry.css:54-61 = cosmetic-dentistry.css:60-64 */
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  /* implant-dentistry.css:62-64 = cosmetic-dentistry.css:65 */
  .eyebrow.light {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }

  /* h2 resolves to ink. Both page sheets reach that colour only through a
     trailing override whose own comment says it works because of its file
     position (cosmetic-dentistry.css:1024); a block that owns its cascade
     states the outcome once.
     Sizing: implant-dentistry.css:87-91 = cosmetic-dentistry.css:73. */
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.2vw, 54px);
  }
  /* `:global()` because the `<em>` arrives through `set:html` and carries no
     scope attribute — a plain `h2 em` compiles to `em[cid]`, misses it, and
     drops the accent colour. Value: implant-dentistry.css:84-86 /
     cosmetic-dentistry.css:77. */
  h2 :global(em) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--vs-sage-soft);
    font-weight: 300;
  }

  /* implant-dentistry.css / cosmetic-dentistry.css:78 (`p` base) */
  p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* implant-dentistry.css:368-400 = cosmetic-dentistry.css:238-260 */
  .section-head {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto 64px;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .section-head .eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
